13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481553 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481553 1083 #uuid "5ae72ad1-700c-5e57-823e-22a80c7c4423" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481554 1080 "2 x + 3 y=18" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481554 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481554 1083 #uuid "5e38e4e5-46ed-537d-af50-ffa565441d62" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481555 1080 "2 x -3 y=-10" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481555 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481555 1083 #uuid "5e450849-303a-5568-9866-2d4dcc0ac383" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481556 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481548 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481556 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481556 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(-3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2, 6)$ and $(-3, 1)$." 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481556 1072 #uuid "80cf21cd-1688-5baa-bf78-1e05cbe212c4" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481556 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,6)$ and $(-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{1-(6)}{-3-(2)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-5}{-5} \\\\ &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-1$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(4) &= -1(x-(-3)) \\\\ y-(4) &= -x -3 \\\\ y &= 1 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= 1 -x \\\\ x + y &= 1 \\\\ 1(x + y) &= 1(1) \\\\ x + y &= 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481549 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481552 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481555 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481559 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1075 #edb/ref 17592186481562 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1077 "selection" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1078 #edb/ref 17592186481549 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481557 1079 #uuid "a04717ff-2885-50c6-ab11-6aff98029a55" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481558 1080 "x -y=-7" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481558 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481558 1083 #uuid "bf84dd2e-d878-51ee-8dc8-9b699953659d" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481559 1080 "2 x -3 y=-8" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481559 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481559 1083 #uuid "c74de8a8-dc89-584b-8e91-b63fb64475a7" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481560 1068 #edb/ref 17592186481557 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481560 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481560 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(5,6)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1).$" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481560 1072 #uuid "c7aa6290-5c44-5145-bf1e-dc13974023ae" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481560 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,5)$ and $(-3,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m_1 &= \\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1} \\\\ &= \\frac{-1-(5)}{-3-(1)} \\\\ &= \\frac{-6}{-4} \\\\ &= \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line. The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(5,6).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(6) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x-(5)) \\\\ y-(6) &= \\frac{10}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y &= \\frac{28}{3} -\\frac{2 x}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2 x}{3} + y &= \\frac{28}{3} \\\\ 3(\\frac{2 x}{3} + y) &= 3(\\frac{28}{3}) \\\\ 2 x + 3 y &= 28\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481561 1080 "\\frac{x}{5} + \\frac{9}{5}" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481561 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481561 1083 #uuid "f1407e8e-66d1-5763-9a7a-aacc749f39bf" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481562 1080 "x + y=11" 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481562 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535223 17592186045443 true] [17592186481562 1083 #uu